Warts - Plantar (Verruca Plantaris)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Hyperkeratotic verrucous papules, Disruption of dermatoglyphics, Thrombosed capillaries/black dots

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-049
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe pain and inability to walk due to sudden localized pressure on a chronic plantar lesion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient experiencing acute, sharp, agonizing pain on the sole of the foot, severely limiting their ability to bear weight or walk. The suddenness and intensity of the mechanical pain raised concern for a retained foreign body or an acutely infected plantar lesion. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate a severe infection from a mechanically irritated plantar wart, and to provide immediate physical symptom relief through paring or offloading, preventing an unnecessary emergency department visit before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There's this hard lump on the bottom of my foot that suddenly feels like I'm stepping on a sharp piece of glass every time I walk."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports noticing a firm, hyperkeratotic nodule on the plantar surface of the right foot several months ago, which they believed was a simple callus. Today, after a long period of standing, the lesion became acutely and exquisitely painful upon direct pressure. The patient presents with a hyperkeratotic exophytic papule with a rough verrucous surface texture. They note pain strictly on direct vertical pressure, completely absent at rest. There is a complete absence of systemic inflammatory symptoms such as fever or chills. The sharp, localized mechanical pain has resulted in a significant limp and functional impairment.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired; hobbling on the lateral edge of the foot to avoid pressure on the lesion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired mobility due to acute mechanical pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Uncomfortable when standing, shifting weight entirely to the left foot.
Physical Exam: On the plantar surface of the right foot, located over the first metatarsal head, there is a 1 cm thick, hyperkeratotic exophytic plaque. Careful inspection reveals a clear disruption of normal dermatoglyphics (skin lines) across the lesion. Using a sterile scalpel blade, a very light paring of the superficial hyperkeratotic layer is performed, immediately revealing multiple pinpoint black dots (thrombosed capillaries). Squeezing the lesion side-to-side produces mild tenderness, but direct vertical pressure elicits sharp, severe pain. There is no surrounding cellulitic erythema, purulent discharge, fluctuance, or localized heat to suggest a deep abscess.
Environmental Observations: Normal home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Plantar Wart (Verruca Plantaris) with acute mechanical irritation - Status: Acute mechanical exacerbation
Differentials:

Callus/Corn (Clavus) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The disruption of normal skin lines across the lesion and the presence of thrombosed capillaries (black dots) upon paring, which are pathognomonic for a viral wart. Corns maintain skin lines and lack vascularity.

Retained Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The superficial paring demonstrating classic viral wart architecture without visualizing a distinct foreign track or material.

Diabetic/Neuropathic Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of sharp, exquisite pain (neuropathic ulcers are painless) and intact protective sensation globally across the foot.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acutely painful plantar lesion characterized by hyperkeratosis, disrupted skin lines, and thrombosed capillaries, confirming a benign Plantar Wart. Triage correctly flagged the acute inability to bear weight and sharp pain as requiring assessment to rule out a deep space infection or retained foreign body. Immediate out-of-hours clinical evaluation was necessary to perform diagnostic superficial paring, which definitively confirmed the viral etiology and safely ruled out an abscess. By performing mild mechanical debridement and applying an offloading dressing, the intervention immediately reduced the sharp mechanical pressure, instantly restoring the patient's ability to walk comfortably and averting a highly unnecessary emergency department presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed gentle bedside paring of the thick overlying hyperkeratotic scale using a sterile scalpel blade to mechanically reduce the mass. Applied a thick, doughnut-shaped (aperture) felt padding directly over the wart to immediately offload the pressure point.
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping this thick, doughnut-shaped pad securely in place, you will physically transfer your body weight away from the sensitive wart, instantly eliminating the sharp, stabbing pain when you walk. By using an over-the-counter salicylic acid paint daily on the exact center of the wart, you will slowly destroy the infected skin cells, permanently curing the root cause of the pressure.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Offloading aperture pads recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP or Podiatry review in 2-4 weeks to commence definitive cryotherapy or chemical destruction if over-the-counter methods fail.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the area suddenly becomes bright red, hot, swollen, or begins weeping thick yellow pus, or if the pain becomes a continuous throb even when resting, present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Plantar warts are famously deeply rooted and stubborn to treat; mechanical offloading manages the pain, but chemical destruction takes weeks.
Clinical Course: The sharp pain upon walking should be immediately and significantly improved by the offloading pad. Total eradication of the wart will require weeks to months of consistent topical treatment.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Ensure the padding does not slip and cause more pressure. Seek medical review if signs of a true bacterial infection (heat, spreading redness) occur.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A common virus has infected the skin on the bottom of your foot, creating a hard lump that acts exactly like a stone in your shoe when you step on it. Paring down the thick skin and using the special padding removed that "stone," stopping the pain.
Actionable Steps: Do not use a pumice stone on the wart, as this can spread the virus to other parts of your foot. Wear thongs in public showers to prevent reinfection.

